# Engineering Quality

- Follow the repository's formatting, naming, tooling, and dependency conventions. Keep the diff focused and use the version and features supported by the project's toolchain.
- Validate untrusted inputs at boundaries. Handle expected failures explicitly, retain diagnostic context for unexpected failures, and use the project's structured logger with redaction.
- Give tasks, subscriptions, timers, controllers, and resources a clear owner. Cancel or dispose them with that owner. Guard against stale responses and handle errors from background tasks. Load expensive resources on demand when appropriate.
- Keep rendering and UI-thread callbacks free of blocking I/O and repeated async work. Batch hot-path lookups, use stable item identities, and measure performance before changing hot paths.
- Run the project's formatter check, analyzer or linter, and relevant tests. Fix diagnostics introduced by the change. Avoid repository-wide automated fixes for a scoped change.
- For behavior changes, exercise the normal entry point and relevant failure paths. Use the current test suite and add a focused regression test when the changed behavior otherwise lacks coverage. Test schema and contract changes using local or test environments.
- For platform or distribution changes, build and smoke-test the affected targets using their supported host or CI configuration. Report which checks ran and which targets still need verification.
- Match shell syntax to the active shell and version. Run dependent commands separately when portability or failure handling is unclear.
